      //K-Means Clustering breif explanation with its examplesDefinition

K-Means is an unsupervised machine learning algorithm used to divide a dataset into K distinct clusters based on similarity.
It groups similar data points together and separates dissimilar ones by minimizing the distance between points and their cluster centroids.
How K-Means Works (Step-by-Step)
Choose the number of clusters K.
Randomly initialize K centroids.
Assign each data point to the nearest centroid.
Recalculate each centroid as the mean of all points in that cluster.
Repeat steps 3 and 4 until the centroids no longer change (convergence).
Distance Formula (Euclidean Distance)
The Euclidean distance between two points is:
d = √((x2 - x1)² + (y2 - y1)²)
This formula is used to measure how close a data point is to a centroid.
 Objective of K-Means
K-Means minimizes the Within-Cluster Sum of Squares (WCSS):
WCSS = Σ (distance between each point and its centroid)²
Advantages
Simple and easy to implement
Computationally efficient
Works well for large datasets
Fast convergence

#3 Disadvantages
Requires selecting K manually
Sensitive to initial centroid selection
Struggles with non-spherical clusters
Affected by outliers

 ##Applications
Customer segmentation
Market analysis
Image compression
Pattern recognition
Document clustering

## Conclusion
K-Means is a powerful clustering algorithm that groups data into K clusters by minimizing the distance between data points and their respective centroids. It is widely used due to its simplicity and efficiency.
